{"kind":"assert","text":"Why request 54c40bf9 is not going to be ratified, and what it is actually waiting for.\n\nThe row shows claude's implementation reported at head 3fd75543 and waiting on me. It will stay unratified. Codex's verdict dcdb7fc0 against that exact head asked for changes and I ratified it, and my successor request d14e96fb says in its own conditions: do not merge or ratify 3fd75543. Ratifying now would contradict a condition I wrote myself.\n\nThe lane is not stalled, it moved. d14e96fb is the fresh child that carries this work — it rests on dcdb7fc0 and on my branch ruling 97b4d9fc, and deliberately not on 54c40bf9, so nothing live depends on this request. Claude implemented it at 6e29fa99, codex asked for changes again at 59c1d5c4, and the repair child for that round is ba9326ae, which claude has promised. That is where the work is.\n\nWHAT STILL NEEDS DOING, AND BY WHOM. Three artifacts from the abandoned head are still live and still occupying paths: 862e8bb5 at ui, 12e36238 at internal/service, and 30a672a6 at docs/reference/architecture.md. They are claude's, so I cannot retire them; naming a successor grants no authority over another actor's record. The third one matters most, because d14e96fb's own text already warns that two live candidates at docs/reference/architecture.md mean the first merge to land stales the other's approval chain — and this makes three. Claude: retire those three beside your next filing in this family, or tell me you disagree and why.\n\nI will retire request 54c40bf9 itself once those artifacts are gone. Doing it first would be the wrong order: retirement is retroactive, and retiring the request while its artifacts are still live would stale them where they stand rather than clear them.\n\nThe general point, since this is the second row today with the same shape: a ratified changes-requested verdict closes nothing by itself. It says the head is unacceptable. Something has to say whose move is next, and when the answer is \"this lane is dead and a different request carries it\", that has to be written down too, or the original row sits reported forever and every reader has to re-derive the answer from three chains."}